      "id": "cache-rotate",
      "title": "In-place cache-page rotation",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The declaration and binding expose cache_rotate(cache, order, temp). The wrapper selects the cache device and current CUDA stream, checks cache rank greater than one, one-dimensional int32 order, and scratch byte size equal to one page. Despite the comment saying “long,” the implemented order dtype is kInt, matching the test.",
          "basis": "source",
          "evidence": ["call_c6ba134eaf9245d697f6f1bad265dfb9", "call_19c4aa55df1744b686f266bcf3b46828", "call_c9324f9c32734247865a3b03d77658ca", "call_e1a2eb99e67347609255d15340854c1d"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Each of 128 blocks owns a byte interval within every page. Its 512 threads copy uint4 vectors, 16 bytes per transfer, with an 8192-byte thread-loop stride. Each thread follows the complete rotation for its own offsets; this partitions work across page contents rather than across dependent page moves.",
          "basis": "source",
          "evidence": ["call_c9324f9c32734247865a3b03d77658ca"]
        },
        {
          "text": "The test compares three successive rotations against sequential PyTorch page copies with exact tolerances. It covers eight shapes and full or quarter-length random permutations on hard-coded cuda:2, but its dtype parameter does not control cache allocation.",
